ontaining ${x}^{10}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{10+9}={x}^{19}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $19.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-46424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "90"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $6,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{6}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{6}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{6+9}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-277896" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277896" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $7$, respectively, what is the degree of $P-Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{7}.$ No ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P-Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{9}.$ No ${x}^{9}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-269922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $15$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-269922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=5{x}^{15}$ and $Q=5{x}^{15}-12.$ Then $P-Q=12,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-269922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-269922" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-269922" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $7.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{7}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{7}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "49"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=4{x}^{3}$ and $Q=4{x}^{3}-10.$ Then $P-Q=10,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-277910" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-277910" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $3{x}^{3}-P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P=3{x}^{3}-1.$ Then $3{x}^{3}-P(x)=3{x}^{3}-(3{x}^{3}-1)=1,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P=-3{x}^{3}.$ Then $3{x}^{3}+P(x)=3{x}^{3}+(-3{x}^{3})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{3}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}={x}^{3+3}={x}^{6},$ meaning that $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $6.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-51690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{6},$ respectively. In $P+Q,$ the leading term still contains an ${x}^{6}.$ There is no ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{6}$ term in $Q.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{6}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-46457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-111390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045c9d12fe2fb42547d82f7d84f41e91789c9cb562c2dd412efaa67e3a8a5edb"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "q-47447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/state.json" "file_sha256" "8959868c72d15b325c9b449573479400d013945389c6c0f8d3f7e6009090e1bd"} {"question" "e-1316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\n- $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\n- $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1316.json" "file_sha256" "2c758816f901d980450e385c5b782d3a307d26e7f7d971177729e3b753ea2b29"} {"question" "e-1316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1316.json" "file_sha256" "2c758816f901d980450e385c5b782d3a307d26e7f7d971177729e3b753ea2b29"} {"question" "e-9981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-9981.json" "file_sha256" "d805fdd5abdc056961d65e11e2168437101c1205f5bb07c45e56214434d3d6e0"} {"question" "e-9981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-9981.json" "file_sha256" "d805fdd5abdc056961d65e11e2168437101c1205f5bb07c45e56214434d3d6e0"} {"question" "e-1317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1317.json" "file_sha256" "fd6d8567ac079870975c6ae7c35d57f922a526ee2c93f33ef5b6c015065fd26b"} {"question" "e-1317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1317.json" "file_sha256" "fd6d8567ac079870975c6ae7c35d57f922a526ee2c93f33ef5b6c015065fd26b"} {"question" "e-1318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\n- $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1318.json" "file_sha256" "f0c25b5878a98899be71605649a692af3a16d6c85954b635f0b27233282f964b"} {"question" "e-1318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040576/example-1318.json" "file_sha256" "f0c25b5878a98899be71605649a692af3a16d6c85954b635f0b27233282f964b"}] "usage" {"e-1317" [] "e-9981" [] "e-1318" [] "q-47447" [] "q-46457" [] "q-141203" [] "e-1316" []} "basis" 1545 "retractions" [[17592186092499 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(x^{2} + 4x) - (-5x^{3} + 3)$\n2. $(2 - x^{3}) \\div (x^{2})$\n3. $(2x - x^{3}) \\cdot (x^{2} - x + 1)$"] [17592186092661 :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P + Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n2. $P - Q$ is always a polynomial\n3. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial"] [17592186091893 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n2. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n3. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$"] [17592186091893 :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n$(5x^{2} - 10x^{3}) \\div (5x)|= \\frac{5x^{2} - 10x^{3}}{5x}; = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = x - 2x^{2}$\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n$\\frac{1 - x^{3}}{x^{3}}|= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}}; = \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - 1$\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186092106 :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q$?"] [17592186092106 :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$, the leading term contains an $x^8$.\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q$, the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing\n\n$$\nx^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10$."] [17592186091894 :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q$?"] [17592186091894 :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3$, the leading term contains an $x^3$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nIn $P-Q$, the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3$. There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P$.\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3$."] [17592186091895 :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n2. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n3. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial."] [17592186091895 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5$. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10}$, meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10$.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5$. Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0$, which is not a polynomial of degree $5$.\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5$. Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1$, which is a polynomial of degree $0$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186089431 17592186089432 17592186089433 17592186089434 17592186089435 17592186090432 17592186090433 17592186090434 17592186090435 17592186090436 17592186294763 17592186294764 17592186294765 17592186294766 17592186294767] "immutable_fields" [[17592186089430 :answer-field/correct] [17592186089430 :answer-field/id] [17592186089430 :answer-field/key] [17592186089430 :answer-field/type] [17592186090431 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090431 :answer-field/id] [17592186090431 :answer-field/key] [17592186090431 :answer-field/type] [17592186294762 :answer-field/correct] [17592186294762 :answer-field/id] [17592186294762 :answer-field/key